# Staged Vault entry history and restoration

This candidate adds automatic previous versions when one of the six editable content fields changes, a history list, explicit masked previews, and confirmed restoration. It is staged under `work/keepass-history-next`; no production files or historical compatibility receipts were changed. Native JavaScript implements the behavior. It imports no upstream implementation and launches no original program in application execution.

Edits to an existing entry copy its previous XML content before changing standard fields. Copies retain protected-string flags, attachment references, tags, Auto-Type metadata, custom fields/data and other XML fields; each copy excludes nested history. A new entry starts without a previous version. A no-op save increments usage and updates last access, while preserving modification time and avoiding an extra history revision. Only title, username, password, URL, notes and the Studio TOTP field are editable in this slice. Group moves retain the pre-existing path behavior and are not modeled as KeePass entry-content changes.

Restore keeps a private copy of the selected revision before making a backup of the current entry and applying limits. It restores entry data and metadata while keeping the current UUID, parent placement, previous-parent UUID, location-change time and maintained history. The new live entry receives the current modification/access time and selected usage count plus one. Limits can prune the selected row during restoration; that does not prevent restoring its already captured data.

The candidate honors imported `HistoryMaxItems` and `HistoryMaxSize` values. Defaults from the released source are10 items and6291456 bytes. Negative values disable the respective original limit. Byte accounting reproduces released `PwEntry.GetSize` for supported flat snapshots, including UTF-16 character counts, raw binary lengths and structural constants. That source formula approximates memory; it is not a measurement of JavaScript or OS memory. Removal follows released unknown-time/oldest-time/first-tie ordering. Ordinary dates on9999-12-31 cannot be pruned by the original sentinel loop; this candidate rejects such required pruning before storage instead of reproducing a nonterminating operation.

The history list returns title/time/index/size and a keyed selection token, with no password, notes or TOTP secret. Explicit preview returns the selected standard fields; the password starts masked. Restore requires a separate confirmation. HMAC-SHA256 tokens use the private local vault key and validate the exact selected snapshot, so changed rows and master-key rotation require a refreshed list. This is a Studio API safeguard, not an original KeePass feature. Returned preview records are copies. Public action input is captured before the serialization queue awaits; accessors and nonplain objects reject. Writes publish session state only after encrypted storage succeeds.

Local bounds remain:10000 main entries,1000 final history revisions per entry,8MiB combined preserved XML/attachment Base64,8MiB XML parsing,100000 XML nodes/depth64, and12MiB captured action input. One pending1001st revision may be created and pruned before the final1000 bound. Unlimited imported histories exceeding that local bound, nested historical histories, malformed snapshot times/settings/duplicate fields/references, and source formats outside the existing native KDBX slice reject before publication. Numeric history settings use canonical signed decimal encoding; broader original parser recovery is not claimed. The shared disk store's16MiB encrypted-record limit can also reject an otherwise supported change atomically.

`history.test.mjs`, `unknown-time.test.mjs`, `http.test.mjs` and `ui.test.mjs` exercise the candidate. The original adapter verifies the preserved release executable's SHA256 before every process and again before assembly load. It executes actual `CreateBackup`, `MaintainBackups`, `RestoreFromBackup`, `GetSize`, `StringToTags` and KDBX reader/writer methods against synthetic data. Source-assisted edit composition and fixed timestamp/usage assignments emulate the relevant dialog path; the original dialog, its event hooks and `Touch` are not executed. GUI execution count is0. DOM tests use an owned small element model, not a browser engine. Five child host starts use exact copied shared HTTP/storage code plus an explicit stage Vault adapter; these are stage HTTP tests, not production integration tests.

`runs/unknown-time-before` preserves the source-stable failing original differential. `runs/unknown-time-after` preserves its correction with eight held-out cases. Other acquisition/adapter/recorder failures and earlier source snapshots stay separate. The final focused receipt records exact source bytes and raw outputs. Existing compatibility18 evidence remains historical and is not rebound to this candidate.

Manual ledger connections are the existing `KPM2611-HISTORY-SNAPSHOT` and `KPM2611-HISTORY-LIST` records. Imported limit enforcement advances part of `KPM2611-HISTORY-COUNT-LIMIT` and `KPM2611-HISTORY-SIZE-LIMIT`; database-settings editing remains open. Selected-history deletion and age-based maintenance remain unimplemented. Restoration is an additional source-backed contract, with its own stage identifier in `contract-map.json`; the supplemental199 ledger remains unchanged.

Not covered: history comparison, original History-tab layout/keyboard behavior, arbitrary metadata editing, group move semantics, database-settings UI, history deletion/age cleanup, undoing an unsaved dialog, original event/plugin integration, Auto-Type execution, full database merge/synchronization, full KeePass parity or original-grade security. No performance superiority or security superiority is claimed. Source-assisted own code is GPL-2.0-or-later; retained selected upstream source and exact original licenses remain under `source/`. Official source archive and executable are local study/oracle artifacts only.
